    "text": "## A structural mechanism for bacterial autotransporter glycosylation by a dodecameric heptosyltransferase family\n\n**DOI:** 10.7554/elife.03714 | **Type:** journal-article\n**Publisher:** eLife Sciences Publications, Ltd\n**Journal/Container:** eLife\n**ISSN:** 2050-084X\n**Published:** 2014-10-13\n**Language:** en\n**Cited by:** 34\n**References:** 64\n**Authors:** showing 11 of 11, starting at index 0\n- Qing Yao (first) — Dr Feng Shao's Laboratory, National Institute of Biological Sciences, Beijing, China\n- Qiuhe Lu (additional) — Dr Feng Shao's Laboratory, National Institute of Biological Sciences, Beijing, China\n- Xiaobo Wan (additional) — Dr Niu Huang's Laboratory, National Institute of Biological Sciences, Beijing, China\n- Feng Song (additional) — National Laboratory of Biomacromolecules, Institute of Biophysics, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, China, Shandong Provincial Key Laboratory of Functional Macromolecular Biophysics, Institute of Biophysics, Dezhou University, Dezhou, China\n- Yue Xu (additional) — Dr Feng Shao's Laboratory, National Institute of Biological Sciences, Beijing, China\n- Mo Hu (additional) — Institute of Analytic Chemistry, College of Chemistry and Molecular Engineering, Peking University, Beijing, China, Synthetic Functional Biomolecules Center, College of Chemistry and Molecular Engineering, Peking University, Beijing, China\n- Alla Zamyatina (additional) — Department of Chemistry, University of Natural Resources and Life Sciences, Vienna, Austria\n- Xiaoyun Liu (additional) — Institute of Analytic Chemistry, College of Chemistry and Molecular Engineering, Peking University, Beijing, China, Synthetic Functional Biomolecules Center, College of Chemistry and Molecular Engineering, Peking University, Beijing, China\n- Niu Huang (additional) — Dr Niu Huang's Laboratory, National Institute of Biological Sciences, Beijing, China\n- Ping Zhu (additional) — National Laboratory of Biomacromolecules, Institute of Biophysics, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, China\n- Feng Shao (additional) — Dr Feng Shao's Laboratory, National Institute of Biological Sciences, Beijing, China, National Laboratory of Biomacromolecules, Institute of Biophysics, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, China\n\n**Abstract:**\nA large group of bacterial virulence autotransporters including AIDA-I from diffusely adhering E. coli (DAEC) and TibA from enterotoxigenic E. coli (ETEC) require hyperglycosylation for functioning. Here we demonstrate that TibC from ETEC harbors a heptosyltransferase activity on TibA and AIDA-I, defining a large family of bacterial autotransporter heptosyltransferases (BAHTs). The crystal structure of TibC reveals a characteristic ring-shape dodecamer. The protomer features an N-terminal β-barrel, a catalytic domain, a β-hairpin thumb, and a unique iron-finger motif. The iron-finger motif contributes to back-to-back dimerization; six dimers form the ring through β-hairpin thumb-mediated hand-in-hand contact. The structure of ADP-D-glycero-β-D-manno-heptose (ADP-D,D-heptose)-bound TibC reveals a sugar transfer mechanism and also the ligand stereoselectivity determinant. Electron-cryomicroscopy analyses uncover a TibC–TibA dodecamer/hexamer assembly with two enzyme molecules binding to one TibA substrate. The complex structure also highlights a high efficient hyperglycosylation of six autotransporter substrates simultaneously by the dodecamer enzyme complex.\n\n**Funders:**\n- Howard Hughes Medical Institute — 10.13039/100000011 (55007431)\n- Ministry of Science and Technology of the People's Republic of China — 10.13039/501100002855 (2012CB518700 and 2014CB849602)\n- National Natural Science Foundation of China — 10.13039/501100001809 (Program for Distinguished Young Scholars 31225002)\n- Chinese Academy of Sciences — 10.13039/501100002367 (XDB08020202)\n- National Natural Science Foundation of China — 10.13039/501100001809 (Program for Distinguished Young Scholars 31230018)\n\n**Licenses:**\n- http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ [vor] (0d embargo)\n- http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ [am] (0d embargo)\n- http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ [tdm] (0d embargo)\n\n**Full-text links:**\n- http://elifesciences.org/lookup/doi/10.7554/eLife.03714 (application/pdf) [text-mining]\n- https://cdn.elifesciences.org/articles/03714/elife-03714-v2.pdf (application/pdf) [text-mining]\n- https://cdn.elifesciences.org/articles/03714/elife-03714-v2.xml (application/xml) [text-mining]\n- https://elifesciences.org/articles/03714 (unspecified) [similarity-checking]\n\n**URL:** https://doi.org/10.7554/elife.03714"
  },
